Step 1: Install AWS CLI

Download the official AWS CLI v2 installer:

https://awscli.amazonaws.com/AWSCLIV2.msi

Install it on your Windows machine. 

==============================================================================

Step 2: Verify Installation

Open CMD or PowerShell and execute:

aws --version

Expected output will resemble:

aws-cli/2.x.x Python/3.x Windows/...
==============================================================================

Step 3: Create an IAM user in AWS

  Open the AWS Management Console.
  Search for IAM.
  Go to Users → Create user.
  Enter a username, for example, local-aws-cli-user.
  Create the user and grant only the permissions it needs.
==============================================================================

Step 4: Create access keys

  Open IAM → Users → local-aws-cli-user.
  Select Security credentials.
  Find Access keys and select Create access key.
  Choose the use case that matches your situation, if prompted.
  Create the key and securely save both values.

You'll receive two credentials:
- Access Key ID — identifies the access key.
- Secret Access Key — the secret used to authenticate requests.  
==============================================================================

Step 5: Configure AWS CLI on your laptop

Open CMD or PowerShell and execute:
   aws configure


Enter the values you created:

AWS Access Key ID: <your-access-key-id>
AWS Secret Access Key: <your-secret-access-key>
Default region name: ap-south-1
Default output format: json
==============================================================================

Step 6: Verify the configuration

aws sts get-caller-identity
